(a) (a) Requirement for a food safety plan.
(a)(1) (1) You must prepare, or have prepared, and implement a written food safety plan.
(a)(2) (2) The food safety plan must be prepared, or its preparation overseen, by one or more preventive controls qualified individuals.
(b) (b) Contents of a food safety plan. The written food safety plan must include:
(b)(1) (1) The written hazard analysis as required by § 117.130(a)(2);
(b)(2) (2) The written preventive controls as required by § 117.135(b);
(b)(3) (3) The written supply-chain program as required by subpart G of this part;
(b)(4) (4) The written recall plan as required by § 117.139(a); and
(b)(5) (5) The written procedures for monitoring the implementation of the preventive controls as required by § 117.145(a);
(b)(6) (6) The written corrective action procedures as required by § 117.150(a)(1); and
(b)(7) (7) The written verification procedures as required by § 117.165(b).
(c) (c) Records. The food safety plan required by this section is a record that is subject to the requirements of subpart F of this part.

(a) (a) Requirement for a hazard analysis.
(a)(1) (1) You must conduct a hazard analysis to identify and evaluate, based on experience, illness data, scientific reports, and other information, known or reasonably foreseeable hazards for each type of food manufactured, processed, packed, or held at your facility to determine whether there are any hazards requiring a preventive control.
(a)(2) (2) The hazard analysis must be written regardless of its outcome.
(b) (b) Hazard identification. The hazard identification must consider:
(b)(1) (1) Known or reasonably foreseeable hazards that include:
(b)(1)(i) (i) Biological hazards, including microbiological hazards such as parasites, environmental pathogens, and other pathogens;
(b)(1)(ii) (ii) Chemical hazards, including radiological hazards, substances such as pesticide and drug residues, natural toxins, decomposition, unapproved food or color additives, and food allergens; and
(b)(1)(iii) (iii) Physical hazards (such as stones, glass, and metal fragments); and
(b)(2) (2) Known or reasonably foreseeable hazards that may be present in the food for any of the following reasons:
(b)(2)(i) (i) The hazard occurs naturally;
(b)(2)(ii) (ii) The hazard may be unintentionally introduced; or
(b)(2)(iii) (iii) The hazard may be intentionally introduced for purposes of economic gain.
(c) (c) Hazard evaluation.
(c)(1)(i) (1)(i) The hazard analysis must include an evaluation of the hazards identified in paragraph (b) of this section to assess the severity of the illness or injury if the hazard were to occur and the probability that the hazard will occur in the absence of preventive controls.
(c)(1)(ii) (ii) The hazard evaluation required by paragraph (c)(1)(i) of this section must include an evaluation of environmental pathogens whenever a ready-to-eat food is exposed to the environment prior to packaging and the packaged food does not receive a treatment or otherwise include a control measure (such as a formulation lethal to the pathogen) that would significantly minimize the pathogen.
(c)(2) (2) The hazard evaluation must consider the effect of the following on the safety of the finished food for the intended consumer:
(c)(2)(i) (i) The formulation of the food;
(c)(2)(ii) (ii) The condition, function, and design of the facility and equipment;
(c)(2)(iii) (iii) Raw materials and other ingredients;
(c)(2)(iv) (iv) Transportation practices;
(c)(2)(v) (v) Manufacturing/processing procedures;
(c)(2)(vi) (vi) Packaging activities and labeling activities;
(c)(2)(vii) (vii) Storage and distribution;
(c)(2)(viii) (viii) Intended or reasonably foreseeable use;
(c)(2)(ix) (ix) Sanitation, including employee hygiene; and
(c)(2)(x) (x) Any other relevant factors, such as the temporal (e.g., weather-related) nature of some hazards (e.g., levels of some natural toxins).

(a) (a) You must validate that the preventive controls identified and implemented in accordance with § 117.135 are adequate to control the hazard as appropriate to the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system.
(b) (b) The validation of the preventive controls:
(b)(1) (1) Must be performed (or overseen) by a preventive controls qualified individual:
(b)(1)(i)(A) (i)(A) Prior to implementation of the food safety plan; or
(b)(1)(i)(B) (B) When necessary to demonstrate the control measures can be implemented as designed:
(b)(1)(i)(B)(1) (1) Within 90 calendar days after production of the applicable food first begins; or
(b)(1)(i)(B)(2) (2) Within a reasonable timeframe, provided that the preventive controls qualified individual prepares (or oversees the preparation of) a written justification for a timeframe that exceeds 90 calendar days after production of the applicable food first begins;
(b)(1)(ii) (ii) Whenever a change to a control measure or combination of control measures could impact whether the control measure or combination of control measures, when properly implemented, will effectively control the hazards; and
(b)(1)(iii) (iii) Whenever a reanalysis of the food safety plan reveals the need to do so;
(b)(2) (2) Must include obtaining and evaluating scientific and technical evidence (or, when such evidence is not available or is inadequate, conducting studies) to determine whether the preventive controls, when properly implemented, will effectively control the hazards; and
(c) (c) You do not need to validate:
(c)(1) (1) The food allergen controls in § 117.135(c)(2);
(c)(2) (2) The sanitation controls in § 117.135(c)(3);
(c)(3) (3) The recall plan in § 117.139;
(c)(4) (4) The supply-chain program in subpart G of this part; and
(c)(5) (5) Other preventive controls, if the preventive controls qualified individual prepares (or oversees the preparation of) a written justification that validation is not applicable based on factors such as the nature of the hazard, and the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system.

(a) (a) One or more preventive controls qualified individuals must do or oversee the following:
(a)(1) (1) Preparation of the food safety plan (§ 117.126(a)(2));
(a)(2) (2) Validation of the preventive controls (§ 117.160(b)(1));
(a)(3) (3) Written justification for validation to be performed in a timeframe that exceeds the first 90 calendar days of production of the applicable food;
(a)(4) (4) Determination that validation is not required (§ 117.160(c)(5));
(a)(5) (5) Review of records (§ 117.165(a)(4));
(a)(6) (6) Written justification for review of records of monitoring and corrective actions within a timeframe that exceeds 7 working days;
(a)(7) (7) Reanalysis of the food safety plan (§ 117.170(d)); and
